(D,"mcq",
"27. During piping system walkdown, a 4-inch CS pipe is found resting on a steel beam with no pipe saddle, clamp, or support as shown on the support drawing. The pipe is approximately 3 metres between supports — the design allows a maximum 2.5 m unsupported span for this pipe size and schedule. What are the implications?",
"No concern — the actual span of 3 m is close to the design maximum and the additional 0.5 m is not significant.\nIssue an NCR — the unsupported span (3 m) exceeds the maximum allowed (2.5 m); excessive span causes overstress and potential sagging, which must be corrected by installing the missing support per the design drawing.\nAccept if the stress analysis engineer confirms the 3 m span is acceptable for the actual operating conditions.\nDocument for correction during the next turnaround; the pipe is unlikely to fail during testing.",
"Issue an NCR — the unsupported span (3 m) exceeds the maximum allowed (2.5 m); excessive span causes overstress and potential sagging, which must be corrected by installing the missing support per the design drawing.",
"Span limitations are based on pipe stress calculations — exceeding the maximum span causes bending overstress and pipe sag. This must be corrected before system acceptance. Accepting based on proximity to the limit or engineering judgment without formal re-analysis is not acceptable.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"28. During piping fabrication, the shop inspector finds that a 10-inch carbon steel pipe has been cold bent to a 5D radius using a mandrel bender. The approved specification requires a minimum bend radius of 5D for shop-fabricated bends. The actual measured centreline radius is 4.8D. What is the finding?",
"Accept — the difference between 4.8D and 5D is within the ±5% tolerance allowed for pipe bending operations.\nIssue an NCR — the 4.8D centreline radius is below the 5D minimum; the bend must be re-evaluated by the stress engineer or rejected and re-fabricated.\nAccept — a tighter radius provides a stronger fitting at the elbow point.\nAccept — the 5D minimum applies to induction hot bending, not cold mandrel bending.",
"Issue an NCR — the 4.8D centreline radius is below the 5D minimum; the bend must be re-evaluated by the stress engineer or rejected and re-fabricated.",
"Minimum bend radius requirements are stress-related — a tighter radius than specified can cause wall thinning, ovality, and stress concentration above design limits. A 4.8D radius (4% below the 5D minimum) must be formally evaluated. No implicit tolerance band exists without a documented procedure. NCR is required.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"29. A process piping isometric shows an eccentric reducer between a 6-inch pipe and a 4-inch pipe with 'flat on top' orientation. The field installation shows a concentric reducer. What is the primary design concern with the substitution?",
"No concern — concentric and eccentric reducers provide equivalent flow performance.\nFlat-on-top eccentric reducers are specified to prevent air accumulation (gas pockets) in liquid lines or liquid slugging in gas lines; concentric reducers create a pocket on one side that can trap gas or liquid depending on service, causing operational issues.\nConcentric reducers are mechanically stronger than eccentric reducers and the substitution is an upgrade.\nFlat-on-top or concentric designation is determined by installation orientation only, not the reducer type; the substitution is acceptable if the reducer is installed flat-side-up.",
"Flat-on-top eccentric reducers are specified to prevent air accumulation (gas pockets) in liquid lines or liquid slugging in gas lines; concentric reducers create a pocket on one side that can trap gas or liquid depending on service, causing operational issues.",
"Eccentric reducer orientation is an engineering design decision based on fluid type: flat-on-top for liquid lines to prevent gas pockets; flat-on-bottom for pump suction. Substituting with a concentric reducer changes the flow geometry and can cause operational problems. This is a design deviation requiring NCR and engineering review.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"30. During pre-test inspection of a piping system, the inspector finds that three isolation blinds specified on the test boundary drawing are missing. The fabricator wants to proceed with testing using only the specified isolation valves. What is the correct action?",
"Accept — isolation valves provide equivalent isolation to blinds for pressure testing purposes.\nHold the test — isolation blinds on the test boundary drawing are mandatory for defining and confirming the test boundary; missing blinds mean the test boundary is not established and the test cannot proceed safely.\nAccept — use blinds only on lines connected to vessels; pipe-to-pipe isolation valves are acceptable.\nProceed with testing at reduced pressure to minimise risk from the missing blinds.",
"Hold the test — isolation blinds on the test boundary drawing are mandatory for defining and confirming the test boundary; missing blinds mean the test boundary is not established and the test cannot proceed safely.",
"Test boundary blinds (spades) are the only reliable method of positive isolation for pressure testing — valves can leak and do not provide the same level of isolation certainty. Missing blinds mean the test boundary is not confirmed and the test procedure cannot be safely executed.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"31. During a line walk of a completed 3-inch CS steam condensate return line, the inspector observes that steam traps are installed at every low point as required. However, the steam trap bodies show no directional flow arrows, and it is unclear whether they are installed in the correct direction. What is the correct action?",
"Accept — steam traps are bi-directional and orientation is not critical.\nHold installation — verify flow direction on each steam trap against vendor documentation and P&ID; reinstall any incorrectly oriented traps; mark direction on the body per the inspection checklist.\nAccept — steam traps are always installed in the vertical position and orientation is not an issue.\nDocument the observation and test the trap operation during commissioning.",
"Hold installation — verify flow direction on each steam trap against vendor documentation and P&ID; reinstall any incorrectly oriented traps; mark direction on the body per the inspection checklist.",
"Steam traps are directional — a backwards-installed trap will not function and may allow steam to bypass into the condensate system or cause water hammer. Verification against vendor data and P&ID is mandatory before acceptance. Commissioning testing is insufficient as the only check.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"32. A SAES-L-350 Class 300 piping system in wet sour gas service requires all flanges to be NACE MR0175 compliant. During MTC review, the flanges are ASTM A105 with a Charpy impact test value of 35 J at −29°C. The NACE MR0175 hardness limit for carbon steel in sour service is HRC 22 maximum. The MTC shows a Brinell hardness of 186 HB. Is this acceptable?",
"Yes — 186 HB converts to approximately HRC 10, which is well below the HRC 22 maximum for NACE MR0175 compliance.\nNo — NACE MR0175 requires a maximum of 200 HB for carbon steel flanges in sour service.\nYes — A105 flanges are pre-approved for sour service in all classes without hardness verification.\nNo — carbon steel flanges in sour service must be tested to HV10, not HRC or HB.",
"Yes — 186 HB converts to approximately HRC 10, which is well below the HRC 22 maximum for NACE MR0175 compliance.",
"NACE MR0175/ISO 15156 hardness limit for carbon steel in sour service is HRC 22 (equivalent to approximately 237 HB or 248 HV10). 186 HB ≈ HRC 10 — well below the limit. The flanges are acceptable for sour service. Distractor B invents a 200 HB limit.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"33. A piping QC inspector signs off a completed spool as 'accepted' and it is sent to the laydown area. Two days later, the stress engineer discovers the spool was fabricated using Schedule 80 pipe instead of the specified Schedule 120. Wall thickness of Sch 80 is 8.56 mm vs. 11.1 mm for Sch 120 (6-inch). What must happen?",
"The inspector must issue an NCR, quarantine the spool, and initiate a formal engineering evaluation of whether Schedule 80 is acceptable for the design conditions before the spool can be released or reworked.\nThe inspector must reject the spool immediately and mandate replacement with Schedule 120 pipe.\nAccept the spool — Schedule 80 may provide sufficient pressure rating for most design conditions.\nRequest the piping stress engineer to confirm the spool can be de-rated and used in a lower-pressure service.",
"The inspector must issue an NCR, quarantine the spool, and initiate a formal engineering evaluation of whether Schedule 80 is acceptable for the design conditions before the spool can be released or reworked.",
"The wall thickness shortfall is a material nonconformance. The spool must be quarantined and the discrepancy formally evaluated. The outcome may be rejection, repair (replacement of the wrong pipe), or concession if the engineering assessment confirms Schedule 80 meets all design requirements — but this must be documented formally, not assumed.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"34. During pipe spool dimensional inspection, the inspector measures a spool fabricated to an isometric with an overall length tolerance of ±3 mm. The measured length is 4.5 mm longer than specified. What is the correct action?",
"Accept — the 4.5 mm error is small and will be accommodated by the flange bolt holes.\nIssue an NCR — the spool is outside the ±3 mm tolerance; it must be assessed for impact on field installation (alignment, flange mismatch, interference) before acceptance or rework.\nAccept if the spool fits between flanges in the field; field fit-up is the ultimate test.\nAccept — dimensional tolerance of ±3 mm applies to individual fittings, not to overall spool length.",
"Issue an NCR — the spool is outside the ±3 mm tolerance; it must be assessed for impact on field installation (alignment, flange mismatch, interference) before acceptance or rework.",
"Dimensional tolerances on fabricated spools are specified to ensure correct field installation. A 4.5 mm overrun exceeds the ±3 mm tolerance and must be formally assessed — the spool may cause interference, misalignment of flanges, or stress on connected equipment nozzles.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"35. During the pre-startup check of a newly installed piping system, the inspector finds that a 6-inch spring hanger support has been installed at an incorrect cold-set position — the spring is fully compressed with no travel range remaining. What is the concern?",
"No concern — spring hangers are designed to accommodate full compression during cold installation.\nMajor concern — a fully compressed spring hanger provides no vertical load absorption during thermal expansion; the pipe will be rigidly supported at that point, creating stress concentrations and potential nozzle overloading at the connected equipment.\nMinor concern — the spring will decompress as the system heats up to operating temperature.\nNo concern — document the as-installed position and verify during the first hot start-up.",
"Major concern — a fully compressed spring hanger provides no vertical load absorption during thermal expansion; the pipe will be rigidly supported at that point, creating stress concentrations and potential nozzle overloading at the connected equipment.",
"Spring hangers must be installed at the specified cold-set position to allow correct travel during thermal expansion (cold to hot). A fully compressed spring cannot accommodate thermal growth and effectively becomes a rigid support, negating its design purpose and potentially overloading equipment nozzles.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"36. An API 570 in-service inspection identifies a 25 mm diameter circular corrosion pit 2.1 mm deep on a 6-inch CS pipe with a nominal wall of 7.1 mm and a minimum required thickness of 5.5 mm. The remaining wall at the pit bottom is 5.0 mm. What is the initial assessment?",
"Accept — the pit bottom remaining wall (5.0 mm) is within the general wall thickness (7.1 mm) and only slightly below minimum required.\nThe pit must be evaluated using the API 579-1/ASME FFS-1 Level 1 or Level 2 assessment for localised corrosion before an accept/reject decision is made.\nReject immediately — any pit penetrating below minimum required thickness mandates pipe replacement per API 570.\nAccept — pits less than 50 mm diameter are excluded from minimum thickness requirements per API 570.",
"The pit must be evaluated using the API 579-1/ASME FFS-1 Level 1 or Level 2 assessment for localised corrosion before an accept/reject decision is made.",
"API 570 para 7.4.3 refers pits and localised corrosion to API 579 FFS assessment. A 5.0 mm remaining wall vs. 5.5 mm minimum required thickness is below minimum, but the small diameter (25 mm) may qualify under FFS Level 1 criteria for localised metal loss — a formal assessment is required before acceptance or rejection.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"37. During pressure testing, the hydrostatic test is completed successfully and the pressure is released. During draining, the inspector observes that water is flowing out of a weep hole in an expansion bellows fitting rather than from the designated drain point. What is the implication?",
"No concern — weep holes in expansion bellows are designed for drainage during testing.\nMajor concern — water flowing from a bellows weep hole indicates an inner sleeve or bellows element has a leak; the bellows must be removed and inspected or replaced before service.\nMinor concern — drain all water before commissioning; the bellows should self-seal under operating pressure.\nNo concern — the expansion bellows weep hole is a test port and will be plugged before operation.",
"Major concern — water flowing from a bellows weep hole indicates an inner sleeve or bellows element has a leak; the bellows must be removed and inspected or replaced before service.",
"Weep holes in expansion bellows are inspection ports, not drainage points. Water discharge from a weep hole during testing indicates the inner convolution or liner is leaking. This is a significant finding requiring investigation — a leaking bellows in service could fail catastrophically.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"38. SAES-L-350 requires all piping connections to compressors and pumps to be subjected to cold-pull or pre-stress (intentional offset) before final bolting of equipment nozzle flanges. During inspection, the piping crew final-bolted the pump nozzle flanges without performing the required cold-pull measurement. What is the correct action?",
"Accept — cold-pull is a good practice but not a mandatory quality hold point in most systems.\nIssue an NCR — cold-pull requirements are specified to manage thermal expansion forces on rotating equipment nozzles; bypassing this requirement risks nozzle overloading and misalignment during operation; the connection must be re-evaluated.\nAccept — cold-pull is only required for piping connected to steam turbines, not pumps.\nDocument and perform the cold-pull measurement during commissioning.",
"Issue an NCR — cold-pull requirements are specified to manage thermal expansion forces on rotating equipment nozzles; bypassing this requirement risks nozzle overloading and misalignment during operation; the connection must be re-evaluated.",
"Cold-pull (pre-stress) is a piping stress analysis design requirement to ensure that thermal expansion does not overload sensitive rotating equipment nozzles during operation. It is not optional. Bypassing it is a procedural nonconformance with engineering consequences.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"39. During a piping system final inspection, the inspector verifies that all small bore piping (≤2 inch) has been secured with adequate clamps per SAES-L-350. The inspector notes several instrument impulse lines spanning 600 mm unsupported between two clamps. SAES-L-350 specifies a maximum 500 mm unsupported span for ¾-inch impulse tubing. What is the finding?",
"Accept — 600 mm is close enough to the 500 mm maximum and within field tolerance.\nIssue an NCR — the 600 mm unsupported span exceeds the 500 mm maximum per SAES-L-350 for ¾-inch tubing; additional clamps must be installed.\nAccept — unsupported span requirements apply to process piping, not instrument impulse tubing.\nDocument and correct at the next maintenance opportunity.",
"Issue an NCR — the 600 mm unsupported span exceeds the 500 mm maximum per SAES-L-350 for ¾-inch tubing; additional clamps must be installed.",
"SAES-L-350 span requirements apply to all piping and tubing including instrument impulse lines. Excessive spans on small bore tubing cause vibration fatigue failures — a well-documented failure mode on instrumentation. The NCR must be corrected before system acceptance.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"40. A Positive Material Identification (PMI) programme requires 10% random sampling of CS pipe fittings on a non-critical utility system. During the PMI, one fitting from the sampled group is found to be SS 304 instead of A105 carbon steel. What is the required sampling expansion per SAES-W-011 when a PMI failure is found?",
"No expansion required — one PMI failure in a non-critical system requires only rejection of the failed fitting.\nDouble the sampling to 20% of remaining fittings in the same batch; if no additional failures are found, accept the batch.\nExpand sampling to 100% of all fittings in the same batch and heat number to establish the true extent of the mix-up.\nQuarantine the entire batch of fittings, issue an NCR, and expand PMI to 100% of the affected batch.",
"Expand sampling to 100% of all fittings in the same batch and heat number to establish the true extent of the mix-up.",
"When PMI identifies a wrong-material fitting, the entire batch from the same shipment/lot is suspect. SAES-W-011 and general PMI programme logic require expanding to 100% of the affected batch to determine whether the failure is isolated or systemic. Quarantine and NCR accompany the expanded testing.",
1),

# ── ESSAY 1–20 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(D,"essay",
"1. During final inspection of a 16-inch Class 600 carbon steel piping spool in H₂S wet sour service, you discover the gaskets installed are Ring Joint (RTJ) style in raised-face (RF) flanges instead of the specified RTJ-in-RTJ groove flanges. The system has been pressure tested and insulation has been applied. Develop a complete NCR response including containment, root cause, disposition options, and corrective actions.",
"","",
"Grade on: RF flanges cannot accept an RTJ gasket properly — the RTJ sits proud on the RF face and creates an eccentric seal that will leak; quarantine and remove insulation to access flanges; NCR scope includes all flanges in the spool; investigate procurement and installation error; disposition: replace all RF flanges with RTJ-grooved flanges and correct gaskets; repeat pressure test; corrective actions for material verification at receipt and installation hold points.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"2. A 3-km buried cross-country pipeline is being commissioned. The hydrostatic test fails due to a pressure drop from 120 bar to 110 bar over a 1-hour hold period. The test pressure is 120 bar (1.5× design). No visible external leaks or pressure relief valve operation are observed. Describe your systematic approach to locating the leak and managing the test failure.",
"","",
"Grade on: Systematic isolation of sections (sectional isolation valves/test points) to locate the leak zone; use of test data (pressure drop rate, location of test instrumentation) to triangulate; nitrogen gas bubble test technique if safe and approved; pigging with instrumented pig (geometry + leak detection) if applicable; CCTV or hydrophone listening on buried sections; NCR documentation for test failure; implications for weld repair and re-test.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"3. A newly constructed piping system is ready for pre-commissioning flushing. The P&ID shows multiple control valves, orifice plates, and a magnetic flowmeter in the line. The construction contractor wants to flush the entire system with water as-installed. Evaluate the risks and describe the pre-flushing requirements and precautions.",
"","",
"Grade on: Control valves (flushing velocity can damage valve trim — must be bypassed or removed); orifice plates (must be removed and replaced with temporary flush spools); magnetic flowmeter (liner may be damaged by debris or excessive velocity — must be bypassed); proper flushing sequence (low point to high point, with vent relief); flushing fluid quality and disposal; documentation of removed/bypassed items and reinstatement checklist.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"4. You are a QC Lead for an ARAMCO piping project. A client audit reveals that 200 spool inspection records are missing hold-point countersignatures from the third-party inspection agency (TPIA). The spools are already in the field, some installed and pressure tested. Describe your recovery plan.",
"","",
"Grade on: Triage the 200 records by risk (critical/safety service vs. utility); arrange for TPIA retrospective review using available objective evidence (photographs, NDE reports, MTCs); for records where evidence is insufficient, consider additional in-situ inspection (UT, PT, dimensional check); issue NCR for the documentation lapse; root cause investigation of oversight gap; client notification protocol; corrective actions to prevent recurrence.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"5. Compare the ASME B31.3 requirements for Normal Fluid Service, Category M Fluid Service (highly toxic), and High-Pressure Fluid Service regarding: (a) weld examination extent; (b) test requirements; (c) material impact testing requirements. Explain why each tier is more stringent.",
"","",
"Grade on: Normal — 5% random RT, hydrotest at 1.5× design; Category M — all welds 100% examination, additional leak test at design pressure, tighter material requirements; High Pressure — 100% volumetric + surface examination, 100% material testing, special design factors, stricter test requirements. Engineering rationale: consequence of failure escalates through tiers (safety hazard, toxic hazard, catastrophic failure).",
20),

(D,"essay",
"6. A process piping QC audit reveals that 40% of flange connections in a completed system were tightened using air-impact wrenches without torque verification. The line is in hydrocarbon service at 80 bar. Describe the investigation and corrective action plan.",
"","",
"Grade on: Risk assessment — which service conditions are affected; potential consequences (under-torque → leak; over-torque → fastener damage, gasket crushing); 100% torque verification using calibrated torque wrench; ASME PCC-1 sequential cross-pattern re-torque on all suspect flanges; flange inspection for gasket damage during improper tightening; pressure test before returning to service; corrective actions for tool control and personnel training.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"7. During an API 570 inspection, you discover that a process piping system in HF (hydrofluoric acid) alkylation service has localised pitting corrosion at two high-flow elbow locations. The measured pit depths are 3.2 mm and 4.1 mm on a 9.5 mm nominal wall pipe with a 6.0 mm minimum required thickness. Describe your evaluation approach and immediate actions.",
"","",
"Grade on: HF alkylation service is extreme consequence — any pipe failure is a major safety incident; remaining wall at pits: 9.5 − 3.2 = 6.3 mm (above min) and 9.5 − 4.1 = 5.4 mm (below 6.0 mm minimum) — second pit is critical; API 579 FFS Level 1/2 assessment for localised metal loss; immediate isolation or derate of the affected section; 100% UT mapping of both elbow zones; notification to process safety and ARAMCO maintenance authority; repair/replacement planning.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"8. A piping QC Inspector discovers that the pipe stress isometrics for a gas compressor discharge line show insufficient flexibility — the thermal expansion analysis predicts nozzle loads exceeding equipment manufacturer limits by 40%. The line is already fabricated and 80% installed. What is your role as QC Inspector and what actions should be taken?",
"","",
"Grade on: QC Inspector's role is to escalate to the engineering team — this is a design deficiency, not a construction deficiency; the inspector should formally document the observation (RFI or technical query); engineering must re-analyse and potentially add expansion loops, flex joints, or re-route sections; already-installed portions may need to be removed and re-fabricated; any decision to proceed with known nozzle overloads requires formal concession and compressor vendor approval.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"9. Describe a comprehensive incoming inspection and material receiving procedure for a large shipment of 200 ASTM A105 carbon steel flanges for an ASME B31.3 piping system. Include documentation checks, physical inspection, and any required testing.",
"","",
"Grade on: Documentation — MTC (heat number, chemical composition, mechanical properties, heat treatment, B16.5 marking); physical inspection — dimensions per ASME B16.5 (bore, OD, bolt circle, bolt hole pattern, raised face or RTJ groove dimensions, flange face finish, markings); material verification — heat number match to MTC, PMI if required by SAES-W-011; visual inspection for defects (cracks, seams, porosity, laps, mechanical damage); storage tagging and segregation per grade.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"10. During final punch-listing of a process piping system before mechanical completion, you identify 45 outstanding punch list items. 12 are Category A (prevent system start-up) and 33 are Category B (complete before start-up). The client's operations representative wants to start commissioning with the Category B items still open. Describe your professional response and the quality implications.",
"","",
"Grade on: Category A items must be resolved before mechanical completion sign-off — these are items that physically prevent safe start-up (e.g., missing blinds, open-ended connections, incomplete pressure tests, wrong valve orientation); Category B items are pre-start-up requirements but may be manageable with a monitored punch list; the QC Inspector cannot sign off mechanical completion with open Category A items; professional escalation through QC Manager to client is appropriate; risk documentation if client overrides.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"11. You receive a complaint from operations that a newly commissioned piping system in cooling water service is vibrating excessively. The cooling water pumps have been checked and are operating within design parameters. Describe your QC-led root cause investigation.",
"","",
"Grade on: Vibration sources — flow-induced vibration (turbulence at reducers, T-junctions, partially open valves), pump mechanical resonance, hydraulic resonance in long pipes; construction-related causes — missing or loose supports, wrong spring hanger set position, resonant pipe span; investigation: check as-built support layout vs. drawing, check all support clamps are tightened, check expansion joint condition, review flow velocity calculations, vibration frequency measurement and comparison to natural frequency of pipe sections.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"12. A piping system in cryogenic LNG service (−165°C) is being constructed using 9% nickel steel pipe. Describe the specific QC inspection programme requirements that differ from standard carbon steel piping construction, covering material, welding, NDE, and testing.",
"","",
"Grade on: Material — 9% Ni steel requires Charpy CVN at −196°C per ASME VIII or equivalent; PMI critical; no carbon steel contact with cryogenic pipe in service; Welding — SAES-W-011 cryogenic filler requirements (ENiCrFe or ERNiCrFe-6), preheat limited (too high causes grain growth), interpass temperature control, no arc strikes; NDE — 100% RT or PAUT, impact-test results on all PQRs; Testing — hydrotest with heated water (>10°C above MDMT) or nitrogen gas test (carefully controlled due to stored energy); post-test drying critical to prevent ice formation.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"13. Describe the complete inspection and test plan (ITP) hold and witness points that should be specified for the installation of a critical 12-inch Class 1500 process piping spool in toxic service, from spool receipt through pressure test acceptance.",
"","",
"Grade on: H(hold) = ARAMCO mandatory stop; W(witness) = ARAMCO attend if possible; R(review) = ARAMCO review records. Points: Spool receipt inspection (MTC, dimensions) — W; Fit-up inspection — H; Pre-weld preheat — W; Root pass visual — H; NDE (100% RT) — H; PWHT (if required) — H; Hardness test — W; Flange face and gasket inspection — W; Bolt torque — W; Pre-test inspection (test boundary, blind list) — H; Hydrostatic test — H; Leak test at design pressure — H; Post-test inspection and reinstatement — W.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"14. A piping system has been commissioned and the first in-service inspection per API 570 is due. Develop a risk-based inspection (RBI) programme for a refinery process piping circuit in high-temperature (350°C) naphthenic acid service. Include probability and consequence factors, recommended inspection methods, and intervals.",
"","",
"Grade on: Probability factors — high-temperature naphthenic acid corrosion rate (typically 1.0–3.0 mm/yr in high-severity service), material vulnerability (CS highly susceptible; SS 316L required), flow-accelerated corrosion at high velocities, process upsets; Consequence factors — inventory, fluid toxicity, proximity to personnel; Inspection methods — UTT, profile RT, TOFD for welds, UT mapping at elbows; Intervals — RBI may justify shorter than API 570 maximum (5-year thickness, 10-year external) based on high corrosion rate; baseline UT within first 3 months of operation.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"15. During construction of an ARAMCO gas plant, a traceability audit reveals that 500 metres of buried carbon steel pipe (NPS 6, Sch 40) has been installed without any anti-corrosion coating. The pipe was specified to have FBE (Fusion Bonded Epoxy) coating. The soil resistivity at the location is 800 Ω·cm (highly corrosive). Describe your response and the implication for the cathodic protection system.",
"","",
"Grade on: Immediate containment — stop further backfilling, issue NCR; excavation of buried sections to verify extent of uncoated pipe; pipe coating must be applied before re-burial (shop-applied FBE preferred; field-applied epoxy mastic may be accepted as alternative with client approval); the cathodic protection system was designed assuming FBE-coated pipe (lower current demand); uncoated pipe in 800 Ω·cm soil will require significantly higher CP current; CP system must be re-designed to accommodate uncoated pipe or cathodic protection adequacy will be compromised.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"16. Compare the pressure testing requirements for piping systems per ASME B31.3 (hydrostatic), ASME B31.4 (liquid petroleum pipelines), and ASME B31.8 (gas pipelines). Highlight key differences in test pressure multiplier, hold time, and leak test requirements.",
"","",
"Grade on: B31.3 — hydrotest at 1.5× design pressure × stress ratio, hold ≥10 minutes, no leaks; B31.4 — hydrostatic test minimum 1.25× MAOP, hold 4–8 hours, no pressure drop; B31.8 — strength test (hydrostatic, pneumatic, or inert gas) at 1.25× MOP, hold ≥8 hours, post-test leak survey; B31.8 also requires class location-based test pressure determination. Key differences: B31.3 focuses on fabrication quality; B31.4/8 include pipeline integrity and serviceability tests with longer hold periods.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"17. During final inspection before mechanical completion, you notice that eight 4-inch gate valves in a process line have their handwheels oriented in the downward (inverted) position. The piping specification requires all gate valves to be installed with handwheels accessible and oriented per the valve manufacturer's recommendation. The valves are as-welded into the spool. Describe your approach to resolving this non-conformance.",
"","",
"Grade on: Document the orientation of all eight valves; issue NCR; consult valve manufacturer's data sheet on acceptable orientations — many gate valves can be installed in any orientation without performance impact, but inverted handwheel increases debris ingress risk in bonnets and makes operation difficult; if the manufacturer permits inverted installation and SAES requirements are met, a concession request may be possible; if not permitted, the valves must be removed, spool sections cut, valves rotated and re-welded, and the affected welds re-inspected.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"18. You are auditing a piping fabrication contractor and discover that the certified weld maps are not being updated in real time — they are filled in at the end of the week from memory. Over 400 welds have been completed without contemporaneous weld map updates. Describe the implications and your corrective action plan.",
"","",
"Grade on: Traceability implications — welds cannot be confidently linked to the correct WPS, welder, inspection records, and NDE reports without contemporaneous records; this is a quality system failure, not just a documentation gap; retrospective reconstruction using NDE reports, photographs, and welder records may be possible but must be verified; unverifiable welds should be subjected to additional NDE; NCR for 400+ welds; systemic corrective action for weld map control procedures and inspector oversight.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"19. A client requests a vendor surveillance visit to a pipe fitting manufacturer to verify that A234 WPB elbows and tees for a Class 600 sour service application are being manufactured to ASTM A234 and SAES-L-350 requirements. Design a comprehensive vendor surveillance checklist covering manufacturing, testing, and documentation.",
"","",
"Grade on: Manufacturing — heat chemistry verification, heat treatment (normalising or quenching/tempering), dimensional inspection per ASME B16.9, surface condition (no laps, seams, cracks), marking per ASTM A234 and MSS SP-25; Testing — tensile (UTS, yield, elongation), Charpy impact if required, hardness per NACE MR0175 for sour service (≤HRC 22), hydrostatic test or NDE of each fitting; Documentation — MTC with all required data, NDE reports, dimensional records, heat treatment records, test results.",
20),

(D,"essay",
"20. A piping system walk-down 6 months after commissioning reveals that 12 spring hanger indicators are outside their operating range (beyond the allowable cold-to-hot travel range). Some are fully extended, others fully compressed. Describe the investigation, implications for system integrity, and corrective actions.",
"","",
"Grade on: Spring hanger travel out of range indicates the pipe stress analysis was incorrect, thermal growth was higher than predicted, or the hangers were installed at wrong cold-set positions; extended hangers suggest pipe is heavier/hot-position is lower than designed; compressed hangers suggest pipe rose more than designed or hanger is restraining upward growth; implications: nozzle overloading at connected equipment, excess stress in pipe, potential for fatigue; corrective actions: re-survey thermal growth, re-analyse stress, re-set hangers, verify equipment nozzle load compliance with manufacturer limits.",
20),

# ── ORAL 1–20 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(D,"oral",
"1. A piping supervisor says 'We've done this flange torquing method on 50 projects and never had a leak.' You know the method he used skips the cross-pattern progressive torquing required by your ITP. What do you do?",
"","",
"Grade on: Professional but firm position — explain that the ITP requirement is based on ASME PCC-1, which is an engineering standard for a reason (uniform gasket seating); experience without failures does not prove the method is code-compliant; the ITP requires progressive cross-pattern torquing, so the torquing must be redone; escalate to QC Manager if supervisor refuses; stop work if flanges are in safety-critical service.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"2. Explain the difference between a hydrostatic test and a pneumatic leak test — when is each used, and what are the safety implications of each?",
"","",
"Grade on: Hydrostatic — uses water (incompressible), higher stored energy efficiency, safe failure mode (no explosion energy), ASME B31.3 default; Pneumatic — uses gas (compressible, high stored energy), explosive failure mode, used only when hydrostatic is impractical (damage to lining, residual liquid hazard), requires exclusion zone and safety plan, lower test pressure (1.1× vs. 1.5×). Safety implication: pneumatic failure releases enormous energy; hydrostatic failure is typically a controlled leak.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"3. You are at a field inspection and notice a pipe spool in hydrocarbon service has a temporary plug (plastic red cap) left in a 2-inch drain nozzle. The spool is about to be hydrotested. What do you do?",
"","",
"Grade on: Stop the test immediately — a plastic cap is not a rated pressure boundary and will fail during the hydrotest; verify all open ends are either properly blinded (spade/blind flange rated for test pressure) or the item to be connected is in place; the plastic shipping cap must be removed and replaced with a proper test blind; check all other nozzles and open ends for similar conditions before authorising the test.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"4. Without referring to a document, explain what NACE MR0175 / ISO 15156 is and its significance for piping material selection in Saudi Aramco facilities.",
"","",
"Grade on: NACE MR0175/ISO 15156 — standard for metallic materials for use in equipment for oil and gas production in H₂S-containing environments; specifies hardness limits, heat treatment requirements, and alloy restrictions to prevent sulfide stress cracking (SSC) and hydrogen-induced cracking (HIC); significance for ARAMCO: Saudi oil/gas fields contain H₂S; piping in sour service must be specified and verified to comply with this standard — wrong material = SSC failure = dangerous leak.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"5. During a pre-test inspection, you notice the system has a DN50 sample connection valve open to atmosphere with no blind flange installed. The test pressure is 80 bar. What do you do?",
"","",
"Grade on: Immediately halt the pre-test activities — an open connection to atmosphere with no blind means the system boundary is breached and pressurising to 80 bar with an open end could project fluid/gas at high velocity, causing injury; require the contractor to install a blind flange rated to ≥80 bar on the open connection before proceeding; verify all open-ended connections on the test boundary drawing have been addressed.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"6. Describe the minimum steps you take to sign off a flange connection as 'accepted' after final bolt torquing.",
"","",
"Grade on: Verify correct gasket type, size, and material per specification; verify gasket centred on flange; verify correct bolt material, grade, length, and lubrication type; check that progressive cross-pattern torquing sequence was performed per approved procedure with calibrated torque wrench; verify final torque value matches the engineering/procedure requirement; verify no scratches or damage on gasket or seating face (before bolting); record all data on inspection record and sign.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"7. The contractor wants to use a ball valve as an isolation device for the hydrostatic test boundary. You know that ball valves are not reliable test boundary devices. How do you handle this?",
"","",
"Grade on: Test boundary isolation must use spades/blinds (blank flanges) — a ball valve is not a positive isolation device for testing purposes (valve seats can leak under test pressure, pressure can bypass through valve body, valve may not be rated for the test pressure condition); explain technically that ASME B31.3 and the test package require blind flanges as test boundaries; insist on the correct hardware; do not approve the test boundary with a valve-only isolation.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"8. Explain what an eccentric reducer is, why 'flat on top' orientation matters, and give an example of when 'flat on bottom' would be specified instead.",
"","",
"Grade on: Eccentric reducer has centreline offset between the two ends; flat on top — the top of the pipe stays level, preventing gas pockets in liquid-filled lines (used in liquid process lines where gas pockets cause flow measurement errors and cavitation); flat on bottom — the bottom of the pipe stays level, preventing liquid slugging in gas lines and avoiding cavitation on pump suction (pump suction reducers are flat-on-bottom to maintain steady liquid fill to the impeller and avoid vapour lock).",
10),

(D,"oral",
"9. During a routine walk-down you notice a piping crew using an adjustable spanner (crescent wrench) to torque a flange bolt on a Class 600 hydrocarbon flange. What is your reaction and why?",
"","",
"Grade on: Stop the work immediately — adjustable spanners apply arbitrary, uncontrolled torque and can over-torque bolts (damaging gaskets, stretching bolts) or under-torque bolts (leak risk); Class 600 hydrocarbon flanges require controlled torque application using a calibrated torque wrench or hydraulic bolt tensioner; document the incident; determine which flanges were tightened with this method and require full re-torquing with the correct tool; issue a toolbox talk on correct practices.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"10. Explain what CUI (Corrosion Under Insulation) is and describe three design/construction measures that reduce CUI risk in a piping system.",
"","",
"Grade on: CUI — corrosion that occurs on the external surface of a pipe beneath insulation, caused by ingress of water (rain, steam leaks, process leaks) that cannot easily evaporate and becomes trapped; temperature range of concern: −4°C to 175°C for CS (austenitic SS susceptible to Cl-SCC between 50–175°C); design/construction measures: (1) use pipe shoes at all support points to prevent water entrapment; (2) use weather-resistant insulation jacketing (aluminium, SS) with sealed joints; (3) weep holes at all support gusset plates; (4) correct insulation end-seal details at flanges and valves; (5) avoid horizontal supports without drainage.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"11. You are reviewing an isometric drawing for a critical high-pressure CS piping system and notice the drawing has no revision history or revision number. The drawing was used for fabrication. What are the implications?",
"","",
"Grade on: An uncontrolled drawing (no revision history) may not be the latest approved version; fabrication from a superseded drawing can result in dimensional errors, wrong specifications, or incorrect materials; implications: the fabricated spool must be verified against the confirmed latest approved isometric; if a revision exists that the fabricator was unaware of, NCR and assessment are required; corrective action for document control system to prevent unapproved drawings reaching the shop floor.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"12. Explain why it is important to verify that a valve is installed in the correct flow direction — give two examples of valve types where direction is critical.",
"","",
"Grade on: Flow direction determines valve function; Examples: (1) Check valve — must face in the direction of permitted forward flow; installed backwards, it fails to prevent backflow (the primary function); (2) Control valve — many control valve bodies are directional; installed backwards, the valve may not regulate correctly, cavitate, or produce excessive pressure drop in the wrong location; (3) Globe valve — installed backwards (flow under disk) may cause seat erosion; (4) Wafer butterfly valve — some designs are asymmetric and must face the pipe flow direction for correct seating.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"13. A field engineer asks you as the QC inspector to sign off a piping spool that is not in your ITP scope. He says it's a routine utility line and the TPIA didn't show up. How do you handle this?",
"","",
"Grade on: The QC inspector cannot sign off work outside their defined scope in the ITP without prior authorisation; explain to the field engineer that signing off work outside the ITP scope is itself a quality system nonconformance; the correct action is to notify the QC Manager, who can either (a) confirm the TPIA's absence was notified and agreed per the ITP procedure, or (b) arrange for the TPIA to attend; if the TPIA hold point can be waived with proper documentation, this must be done formally — not by having the QC inspector sign in place of the TPIA.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"14. Explain the concept of 'stress corrosion cracking' (SCC) and identify two service environments in a piping system where SCC is a significant concern.",
"","",
"Grade on: SCC — corrosion mechanism that requires the simultaneous presence of three conditions: (1) susceptible material, (2) tensile stress (residual or applied), (3) specific corrosive environment; the crack propagates under combined stress and corrosion even though neither alone would cause rapid failure; Examples: (1) Austenitic SS (304/316) in chloride-containing environments — Cl-SCC at >60°C; (2) Carbon steel in caustic (NaOH) — caustic SCC (stress corrosion cracking also called caustic embrittlement); (3) Carbon/low-alloy steel in wet H₂S — sulfide stress cracking (SSC); (4) Duplex SS in polythionic acid environments during shutdowns.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"15. You arrive to inspect a line flushing operation and notice the operator is flushing a stainless steel process line with portable water from a garden hose. The water source is not the approved demineralised water specified in the flushing procedure. What do you do?",
"","",
"Grade on: Stop the flushing immediately — potable water contains chlorides (typically 50–250 ppm Cl) that remain on the pipe surface after drying and can initiate chloride stress corrosion cracking in stainless steel under operating conditions; the flush must be completed using demineralised or distilled water as specified; the line must be inspected for residual contamination (Cl test strips if already flushed with potable water); issue NCR; corrective action for flushing medium control.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"16. What is the purpose of a spectacle blind (figure-8 blind) in piping, and how does it differ from a spade and a spacer ring?",
"","",
"Grade on: Spectacle blind — a figure-8 shaped fitting with one solid half (blind) and one open ring half (spacer); it can be rotated to switch between isolated (blind side in) and open (spacer side in) configurations, with the position visible externally; Spade — solid disc only (blind); used for long-term isolation; Spacer — open ring only; keeps flanges at full bolt length when the blind is not in use; Practical use: spectacle blinds are ideal for operations that regularly switch between isolated and normal flow, providing clear visual indication of blind status — critical for safety permit-to-work systems.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"17. Describe the sequence of events that should happen when a pressure test fails — who gets notified, what documentation is generated, and how is the repair managed?",
"","",
"Grade on: Sequence: (1) Depressurise safely per test procedure; (2) Notify: QC Manager, Construction Manager, TPIA inspector, ARAMCO resident engineer; (3) Issue NCR documenting the test failure, observed symptoms, and location of leak (if found); (4) Investigate root cause — find leak location, characterise failure (fabrication defect, wrong material, missing seal, etc.); (5) Develop repair plan (weld repair, gasket replacement, valve replacement); (6) Implement repair under normal QC hold-point regime; (7) Repeat pressure test; (8) Document test repeat and acceptance; NCR formally closed with evidence.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"18. Explain the difference between a butt weld fitting and a socket weld fitting — when would you use each, and what are the ASME B31.3 restrictions on socket welds?",
"","",
"Grade on: Butt weld — pipe ends meet face-to-face with a full-penetration groove weld; provides full bore with no step; used for all sizes in critical service; inspectable by RT/UT; Socket weld — pipe slips into the fitting socket; sealed by fillet weld only; creates a crevice at the pipe-socket junction; limited to NPS ≤ 2 inch per ASME B31.3; not permitted in severe cyclic service; crevice makes RT of root impossible; used for small bore less critical applications.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"19. During a hydrostatic test walk-down, you notice a pressure gauge has a cracked lens (the glass face is cracked but the needle is visible). The gauge is being used to monitor test pressure. What is your action?",
"","",
"Grade on: Stop and replace the gauge — a cracked lens is a safety hazard (the glass can shatter under pressure, injuring personnel) and an instrumentation integrity concern (the crack may have been caused by over-pressure, suggesting the gauge mechanism may be damaged and reading incorrectly); replace with a calibrated gauge with current calibration sticker; investigate whether over-pressure may have occurred; record the replacement in the test documentation.",
10),

(D,"oral",
"20. A construction crew is about to install pipe in a trench. You observe the trench bottom has standing water and the bedding material is coarse gravel (instead of the specified fine sand). The pipe is FBE-coated. What are your concerns and actions?",
"","",
"Grade on: Two concerns: (1) Coarse gravel bedding will damage the FBE coating — hard angular gravel contacts create stress concentration points that chip or penetrate the coating; fine sand/granular fill is specified to protect the coating; (2) Standing water in the trench — laying pipe in water risks coating damage, weld contamination, and improper bedding compaction; Actions: stop pipe installation; require dewatering before placement; require replacement of coarse gravel with specified fine sand/granular fill per SAES coating specification; inspect the FBE coating after bedding correction for any damage using holiday detector.",
10),

# ── PRACTICAL 1–20 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(D,"practical",
"1. You receive a hydrostatic test package for a 4-inch Class 600 CS process piping system. The test data shows: Design pressure 50 bar, design temperature 300°C, allowable stress at 300°C (S) = 117 MPa, allowable stress at ambient (St) = 138 MPa. Calculate the minimum required test pressure per ASME B31.3 para 345.4.2 and state whether the proposed test pressure of 90 bar is acceptable.",
"","",
"Grade on: Formula: Pt = 1.5 × P × (St/S) = 1.5 × 50 × (138/117) = 75 × 1.179 = 88.4 bar minimum. Proposed test pressure = 90 bar. 90 bar > 88.4 bar — ACCEPTABLE. Candidate must correctly apply the stress ratio correction; the common error is calculating 1.5 × 50 = 75 bar without the temperature correction factor.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"2. Review the following flange inspection record and identify any discrepancies: Flange A (6-inch Class 300 RF): Gasket type — spiral-wound SS/PTFE; Face finish measured — 3.2 μm Ra (specified ASME B16.5 range 3.2–6.3 μm Ra for spiral-wound gaskets); Two radial scratches 0.1 mm deep, 30 mm long across the raised face; Bolts — A193 B7 studs with A194 2H nuts (correct per specification); Torque — 180 Nm (single pass).",
"","",
"Grade on: (1) Gasket type — spiral-wound SS/PTFE for Class 300 RF — ACCEPTABLE (verify inner ring required for Class ≥300 per ASME B16.20); (2) Face finish 3.2 μm Ra — at the lower boundary of the acceptable range (3.2–6.3 μm Ra for ASME B16.5 with spiral-wound gasket) — borderline ACCEPTABLE; (3) Two radial scratches 0.1 mm deep across the raised face — REJECT — radial scratches are leak paths, must be re-machined; (4) Bolt material — correct; (5) Torque — 180 Nm single pass — REJECT per ASME PCC-1 — must be progressive cross-pattern torquing (30%, 70%, 100%). Two NCR items.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"3. Calculate the remaining life and next inspection date for the following API 570 data: Current wall thickness = 6.8 mm; Minimum required thickness = 4.5 mm; Corrosion rate (short-term) = 0.6 mm/yr; Corrosion rate (long-term) = 0.4 mm/yr. API 570 requires using the higher of the two rates. State the remaining life and next inspection interval.",
"","",
"Grade on: Use the higher corrosion rate = 0.6 mm/yr. Remaining life = (6.8 − 4.5) / 0.6 = 2.3 / 0.6 = 3.83 years. Next inspection interval per API 570 = half the remaining life = 1.92 years (approximately 23 months). The inspection must also not exceed the maximum allowed interval per API 570 for the risk category (typically 10 years for external, 5 years for internal — but remaining life governs here). Next inspection due in approximately 1.9 years.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"4. You receive an MTC for a shipment of ASME B16.5 Class 600 A105 carbon steel flanges. Verify the following MTC data against ASTM A105 requirements: C=0.34%, Mn=0.85%, Si=0.25%, P=0.022%, S=0.004%, Cu=0.25%, Ni=0.32%, Cr=0.22%, Mo=0.11%, V=0.03%, Nb=0.01%, CE (IIW)=0.48%. Tensile strength=510 MPa, Yield=265 MPa, Elongation=25%.",
"","",
"Grade on: ASTM A105 chemistry limits — C ≤0.35 ✓; Mn 0.60–1.05 ✓; Si 0.10–0.35 ✓; P ≤0.035 ✓; S ≤0.040 ✓; Cu ≤0.40 ✓; Ni ≤0.40 ✓; Cr ≤0.30 ✓; Mo ≤0.12 ✓; V ≤0.05 ✓; Nb ≤0.02 ✓. Mechanical: UTS ≥485 MPa ✓ (510 >485); Yield ≥250 MPa ✓ (265 >250); Elongation ≥22% ✓ (25 >22). All values within ASTM A105 requirements — MTC ACCEPTABLE. CE 0.48 — confirm against any project preheat requirements.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"5. You are performing a dimensional check on a fabricated 6-inch Schedule 80 pipe spool (isometric tolerance ±3 mm). Measured data: Overall face-to-face length 4,503 mm (drawing 4,500 mm); Flange centre-to-end distance leg A: 1,251 mm (drawing 1,250 mm); Flange centre-to-end distance leg B: 1,252 mm (drawing 1,250 mm); Out-of-square of flange A face: 1.5° (max allowed 1°). Evaluate each dimension.",
"","",
"Grade on: (1) Overall F-to-F length 4,503 mm vs. 4,500 mm — difference 3 mm — at the tolerance limit — ACCEPTABLE (borderline — flag for fit-up verification in field); (2) Leg A: 1,251 mm vs. 1,250 mm — difference 1 mm — ACCEPTABLE; (3) Leg B: 1,252 mm vs. 1,250 mm — difference 2 mm — ACCEPTABLE; (4) Out-of-square flange A: 1.5° vs. 1° maximum — FAIL — flange face not perpendicular to pipe axis within tolerance; NCR required for flange re-cutting or spool rejection.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"6. Review a piping test package and identify all discrepancies: Test package for P&ID No. ML-4501; Test pressure: 60 bar (1.5× design of 40 bar); Test medium: Water; Blind list: 12 blinds listed; Field verification: 10 blinds installed, 2 missing; Test gauge range: 0–100 bar; Test gauge calibration: current; Hold time: 30 minutes; SRV set pressure in system: 55 bar (SRV not blinded).",
"","",
"Grade on: (1) 2 blinds missing from 12 listed — FAIL — test boundary incomplete; test must be halted; (2) Gauge range 0–100 bar for 60 bar test — check ASME B31.3 requirement (1.5–4× test pressure = 90–240 bar range) — 100 bar gauge maximum is above the test pressure but below 1.5× the test pressure (90 bar) — BORDERLINE — 100 bar gauge is acceptable as 1.5× test pressure is 90 bar (within range); (3) SRV set pressure 55 bar < test pressure 60 bar — SRV not blinded — FAIL — SRV will open at 55 bar, preventing achievement of 60 bar test pressure. Two fails: missing blinds and un-blinded SRV.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"7. A UTT survey of a 4-inch Class 300 CS pipe elbow in a corrosive service shows the following readings at a high-wear point: Reading 1: 8.2 mm; Reading 2: 8.0 mm; Reading 3: 7.3 mm; Reading 4: 6.1 mm; Reading 5: 5.4 mm; Reading 6: 4.9 mm. Nominal wall thickness: 8.6 mm; Minimum required thickness: 5.5 mm. Corrosion allowance: 3.1 mm. Identify the critical reading(s) and state what action is required.",
"","",
"Grade on: Critical reading: Reading 6 at 4.9 mm < minimum required 5.5 mm — this location is below minimum thickness and requires immediate action. Reading 5 at 5.4 mm is also below minimum (5.4 < 5.5) — requires action. Reading 4 at 6.1 mm is above minimum — acceptable but close. Required actions: Immediate FFS assessment per API 579 for readings 5 and 6; consideration of deration or removal from service; expanded UTT mapping around the failed locations to determine extent; NCR and API 570 reporting to ARAMCO maintenance authority.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"8. Inspect the following piping material receiving inspection checklist for completeness and flag missing items for a shipment of 50 ASTM A312 TP316L stainless steel seamless pipes (6-inch, Sch 10S): MTC received: Yes; Heat number on pipes matches MTC: Verified for 20 of 50 pipes; OD and wall thickness measured: Yes; Length recorded: Yes; Visual inspection for surface defects: Yes; Pressure test per ASTM A312: Confirmed on MTC; PMI performed: No; Pipe marking includes: ASTM A312, TP316L, heat number.",
"","",
"Grade on: Missing/incomplete items: (1) Heat number verification only 20/50 (40%) — should be 100% for SS material or at minimum match project PMI plan; (2) PMI not performed — SAES-W-011 requires 100% PMI for all SS materials before fabrication — this is a nonconformance; (3) Checking for end-bevel condition (weld preparation) not listed; (4) No squareness/perpendicularity check of pipe ends; (5) No check of internal surface condition (for seamless pipe, internal surface defects are relevant); (6) Hydrostatic pressure test — confirmed on MTC (acceptable — not required to repeat). Main findings: 100% heat number verification required; PMI missing — NCR.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"9. Review the following piping ITP entry and identify any hold/witness/review point designation errors for a Class 600 CS process pipe in toxic service: (a) Material receiving inspection — R (Review); (b) Spool fit-up and weld joint prep — W (Witness); (c) NDE (100% RT) — R (Review); (d) Hydrostatic test — W (Witness); (e) Flange torquing — R (Review); (f) Final dimensional check — R (Review).",
"","",
"Grade on: For toxic service Class 600 piping: (a) Material receiving — should be W (Witness) or H (Hold) to verify MTC and PMI; R is insufficient; (b) Fit-up — W is appropriate; (c) 100% RT for toxic service — should be H (Hold) not R; RT of toxic service piping requires ARAMCO/TPIA physical attendance or at minimum a formal film review hold point; (d) Hydrostatic test for toxic service — should be H (Hold), not W; (e) Flange torquing — R is marginally acceptable but W would be more appropriate for toxic service; (f) Final dimensional — R is acceptable. Corrections needed: items (a), (c), and (d) should be upgraded.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"10. You receive a piping spool for a 4-inch CS steam line. The spool label shows: Material: ASTM A106 Gr.B, Sch 40; Heat number: 2026-PH-442; Drawing No.: STE-4501 Rev.3; QC release: Signed. However, the ITP requires a PWHT record for all steam line welds. Examine the weld documentation package and list all records that should be present before signing the spool for release to field installation.",
"","",
"Grade on: Required records: (1) WPS and PQR reference for each weld in the spool; (2) Welder qualification records (WPQ) for all welders who worked on the spool; (3) Fit-up inspection records for each weld joint; (4) Preheat records for each weld; (5) PWHT chart recording for each weld (mandatory per ITP); (6) NDE reports (RT or UT as per ITP); (7) Visual inspection records; (8) Dimensional check record; (9) MTC for pipe, fittings, and filler materials; (10) PMI records if required by SAES. Any of these absent = spool should not be released.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"11. A pipe stress engineer provides you with these nozzle loads for a pump: Actual loads (from stress analysis): Fx=4.2 kN, Fy=5.8 kN, Fz=2.1 kN, Mx=0.8 kN·m, My=1.2 kN·m, Mz=0.5 kN·m. Manufacturer allowable nozzle loads (discharge nozzle): Fx=3.5 kN, Fy=6.0 kN, Fz=3.0 kN, Mx=1.0 kN·m, My=1.5 kN·m, Mz=0.8 kN·m. Evaluate the nozzle load compliance and state the finding.",
"","",
"Grade on: Compare actual vs. allowable for each load component: Fx: 4.2 vs. 3.5 kN — FAIL (4.2 > 3.5); Fy: 5.8 vs. 6.0 kN — PASS; Fz: 2.1 vs. 3.0 kN — PASS; Mx: 0.8 vs. 1.0 kN·m — PASS; My: 1.2 vs. 1.5 kN·m — PASS; Mz: 0.5 vs. 0.8 kN·m — PASS. Finding: Fx EXCEEDS allowable by 0.7 kN (20% over). The piping system cannot be accepted — the stress analysis must be revised to reduce the Fx load (add flexibility, re-route, adjust support positions). NCR or RFI to stress engineer required.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"12. Review the following pipe schedule for an 8-inch CS process line in sour service and identify any specification violations per SAES-L-350 and NACE MR0175: Pipe: ASTM A53 Gr.B, ERW; Fittings: ASTM A234 WPB; Flanges: ASTM A181 Cl.70; Bolts: ASTM A307 Gr.B; Gaskets: Compressed asbestos-fibre (CAF); Valves: A216 WCB body, TFE seats; Design hardness limit: HRC 22.",
"","",
"Grade on: (1) A53 Gr.B ERW — ERW pipe (electric resistance weld) is generally not preferred for sour service per SAES-L-350 (seamless preferred); (2) A181 Cl.70 flanges — ASTM A181 is a forging standard but Cl.70 is a lower grade — should be A105 for sour service flanges (A181 may not meet hardness requirements — verify); (3) A307 Gr.B bolts — A307 is not in ASTM A193 required for ASME B16.5 Class 150+; SAES requires A193 B7/A194 2H for pressure flanges; (4) CAF gaskets — asbestos-free required; compressed asbestos-fibre is prohibited. Multiple nonconformances.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"13. An as-built isometric of a 3-inch CS instrument air line shows that a globe valve is installed in a vertical orientation with the stem pointing downward. The drawing shows the stem should be horizontal. You also observe the valve handwheel is accessible but the actuator (manual gear operator) is missing its locking pin. Identify all findings.",
"","",
"Grade on: (1) Globe valve stem downward in vertical orientation — some globe valve manufacturers prohibit or discourage inverted-stem installation (debris and packing damage); verify against manufacturer's installation manual for this specific valve; if not permitted, NCR for re-installation per manufacturer guidance; if permitted, accept with note; (2) Gear actuator missing locking pin — the locking pin prevents unintended operation of the gear drive; missing locking pin is a safety finding — the valve position could change inadvertently; NCR to have locking pin installed per manufacturer's instructions before system is placed in service.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"14. During spring hanger pre-commissioning inspection, you record the following cold-set indicator positions vs. specified cold-set positions for 5 spring hangers: Hanger 1: Specified cold-set 45 mm (compressed), Actual 45 mm — OK; Hanger 2: Specified 60 mm, Actual 80 mm — elongated; Hanger 3: Specified 50 mm, Actual 50 mm — OK; Hanger 4: Specified 55 mm, Actual 20 mm — compressed more than specified; Hanger 5: Specified 65 mm, Actual 65 mm — OK. Evaluate and state findings.",
"","",
"Grade on: (1) Hanger 1 — correct; (2) Hanger 2 — 80 mm vs. 60 mm specified = 20 mm more elongated than specified — FAIL — hanger is carrying less load than designed (pipe heavier on this support at cold position); possible incorrect installation or wrong spring selected; (3) Hanger 3 — correct; (4) Hanger 4 — 20 mm vs. 55 mm specified = 35 mm more compressed than specified — FAIL — hanger is over-loaded (pipe heavier than designed at this point) or wrong spring selected; risk of spring bottoming out during thermal expansion; (5) Hanger 5 — correct. NCR for hangers 2 and 4 — inspect spring selection and reinstall per design cold-set positions.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"15. Review the following test boundary document for a hydrostatic test and confirm whether the test is ready to proceed: Test pressure: 75 bar; Test medium: Fresh water; Gauge range: 0–150 bar, calibration current; Blind list: 8 blinds — all installed and inspected; SRVs in system: 2 SRVs (both removed and blanked); Expansion joints: 1 bellows expansion joint (pressure-isolated with tie rods installed); Instruments: 2 pressure transmitters disconnected and blinded; Vents: 2 vents open at highest point for air removal; Test engineer signature: Present; TPIA witness: Confirmed present.",
"","",
"Grade on: Check all items: (1) Test pressure 75 bar — verify calculation (1.5 × P × St/S) — cannot confirm without design data but note for verification; (2) Gauge 0–150 bar for 75 bar test — 1.5× test = 112.5 bar minimum range; 4× test = 300 bar maximum; 150 bar is within range — OK; (3) All 8 blinds installed — OK; (4) SRVs removed and blanked — OK; (5) Expansion joint with tie rods — tie rods must be installed before hydrostatic test to prevent bellows over-extension under water pressure — OK; (6) Instruments disconnected and blinded — OK; (7) Vents open — correct for air removal; (8) Signatures confirmed — OK. READY TO PROCEED — all items accounted for. (Candidate must check all items systematically.)",
15),

(D,"practical",
"16. A contractor submits a flange bolt torque calculation for 8-inch Class 600 ANSI flanges using A193 B7 stud bolts, spiral-wound gasket with inner ring. They calculated a target torque of 340 Nm. During your review you note: bolt size is 7/8-inch diameter, number of bolts is 12, and the calculation uses a K-factor (nut factor) of 0.2. A colleague says the K-factor should be 0.15 for lubricated bolts with Molykote. Evaluate the torque calculation sensitivity to this K-factor difference.",
"","",
"Grade on: Torque T = K × F × D, where K = nut factor, F = bolt load, D = nominal bolt diameter. The K-factor directly and linearly affects the required torque — a K of 0.15 vs. 0.20 changes torque by (0.15/0.20) = 75%; if 340 Nm was calculated at K=0.20 with Molykote (which should use K=0.15), the actual torque achieves a 33% HIGHER bolt load than intended (same torque, lower K = more preload). Conversely, if K=0.15 is correct, using K=0.20 torque would underload the bolts. The calculation should use the K-factor for the actual lubricant applied. This is a design issue requiring engineering clarification and revision of the torque procedure with the correct K-factor.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"17. During line flushing of a new 6-inch CS process line, the flushing team reports that water exiting from the downstream flush point is milky white and turbid. The line was internally pickled with citric acid solution before flushing. Evaluate the flushing status and state what action is required.",
"","",
"Grade on: Milky white turbid water after citric acid pickling can indicate: (1) Residual acid reacting with pipe scale or iron oxide, producing iron citrate compounds; (2) Insufficient rinsing — acid not fully displaced; (3) Possible surface passivation reaction products; Action required: Continue flushing with clean water until the outlet runs clear and pH matches the inlet flush water pH (within ±0.5 pH); test pH and conductivity of flushing water outlet; if still turbid after extended flushing, investigate whether pickling solution concentration or contact time was correct; do not place line in service until flushing water is clear and meets acceptance criteria (pH, conductivity, turbidity).",
15),

(D,"practical",
"18. A piping inspection report shows a 3-inch SS316L pipe elbow at a dead-leg junction in a pharmaceutical utility (high-purity water, USP grade) service. The elbow creates a dead-leg of 350 mm length from the last T-junction. The project specification states dead-legs must not exceed 3× the pipe diameter (3D rule). Evaluate whether this dead-leg is acceptable.",
"","",
"Grade on: 3× pipe diameter rule: pipe ID approximately 73 mm for 3-inch Schedule 10S (OD = 88.9 mm, wall = 2.77 mm, ID ≈ 83.4 mm). 3D = 3 × 83.4 = 250 mm. Dead-leg length = 350 mm. 350 mm > 250 mm — FAIL the 3D rule. The dead-leg exceeds the maximum allowed length. In high-purity water service, excessive dead-legs create stagnant zones where biofilm growth (microbiological contamination) can occur. The piping must be redesigned to shorten the dead-leg or re-route to eliminate it.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"19. You inspect a completed insulated piping system and notice the aluminium jacketing has circumferential laps that face upward (open side facing up, allowing rain ingress). The specification requires all jacketing laps to face downward to shed water. Assess the finding and describe the remediation.",
"","",
"Grade on: Upward-facing jacketing laps are a direct moisture ingress path that will cause CUI — in the Arabian Gulf climate, even occasional rain events and process steam condensation can cause rapid corrosion under insulation over years; this is a specification deviation; NCR required; remediation: all jacketing sections with upward laps must be removed and reinstalled with correct downward lap orientation; inspect underlying insulation for moisture damage before reinstatement; ensure correct lap orientation for all future jacketing installation.",
15),

(D,"practical",
"20. You receive a test package for a pneumatic leak test of a completed instrument air distribution ring main (8 bar design pressure, 12 bar test pressure). Review the following test preparation checklist and identify any safety or procedural deficiencies: Test medium: Nitrogen; Exclusion zone: 3 metres; Test pressure: 12 bar; Test gauge: 0–25 bar, calibrated; Safety relief valve: Set at 13 bar; Personnel in exclusion zone: 2 workers performing visual inspection during pressurisation; Test increments: Direct to 12 bar.",
"","",
"Grade on: (1) Personnel in exclusion zone during pressurisation — CRITICAL SAFETY FAILURE — no personnel should be in the exclusion zone during pressurisation of a pneumatic test; inspection at pressure must be performed remotely or after pressure hold from outside the zone; (2) Direct to 12 bar without incremental pressurisation — FAIL — ASME B31.3 para 345.5 requires pneumatic tests to be performed in incremental steps (e.g., 50%, 75%, 100%) with hold at each increment for inspection; (3) Exclusion zone of 3 metres may be insufficient for a 12 bar pneumatic test on a ring main — engineering should calculate the exclusion zone based on stored energy. Immediately STOP and require test procedure revision.",
15),

